WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.990 --> 00:00:03.660
MARTIN: Hey, [? Meta. ?] I heard
you built a cool application

00:00:03.660 --> 00:00:04.920
recently.

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:06.030
SPEAKER: Hi, Martin.

00:00:06.030 --> 00:00:10.290
Yes, my colleague Guillaume
and I built a serverless app.

00:00:10.290 --> 00:00:12.600
It has multiple [INAUDIBLE]
services communicating

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:15.000
in a nice choreography.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:16.620
MARTIN: Choreography?

00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:18.690
Can you explain that a little?

00:00:18.690 --> 00:00:19.890
SPEAKER: Sure.

00:00:19.890 --> 00:00:21.900
When you have a set of
[INAUDIBLE] services,

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:23.610
how do you get those
[INAUDIBLE] services

00:00:23.610 --> 00:00:26.880
to interact with each
other toward a common goal?

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:30.240
There are two approaches,
choreography and orchestration.

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:33.234
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:39.730 --> 00:00:43.420
In choreography, each
service works independently

00:00:43.420 --> 00:00:45.940
and interacts with other
services in a loosely coupled

00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:48.490
way through events.

00:00:48.490 --> 00:00:51.860
In orchestration, there
is a central orchestrator

00:00:51.860 --> 00:00:55.760
controlling all interactions
between services.

00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:58.550
In our app called
[? Picadaily, ?] we

00:00:58.550 --> 00:01:01.070
decided to go with choreography.

00:01:01.070 --> 00:01:03.980
MARTIN: [? Picadaily, ?]
what does that mean?

00:01:03.980 --> 00:01:05.540
SPEAKER: Good question.

00:01:05.540 --> 00:01:07.340
It's a picture-sharing app.

00:01:07.340 --> 00:01:11.180
We built it as a prototype
for a customer in London.

00:01:11.180 --> 00:01:13.970
We combined Picadilly
Circus from London

00:01:13.970 --> 00:01:17.570
with picture sharing and decided
to call it [? Picadaily. ?]

00:01:17.570 --> 00:01:18.860
MARTIN: Oh, cool.

00:01:18.860 --> 00:01:22.080
Can I see a demo, [INAUDIBLE]?

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:22.890
SPEAKER: Of course.

00:01:22.890 --> 00:01:24.340
Let's take a look.

00:01:24.340 --> 00:01:27.280
I already have some pictures
uploaded in the app.

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:31.300
Under each picture, you
can see some labels.

00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:35.650
These are labels extracted
by Vision API telling us

00:01:35.650 --> 00:01:37.650
what the picture is about.

00:01:37.650 --> 00:01:41.550
Around each picture, you can
also see a colored border.

00:01:41.550 --> 00:01:45.090
This is the dominant color of
the picture, again extracted

00:01:45.090 --> 00:01:47.380
by the Vision API.

00:01:47.380 --> 00:01:51.650
The app also creates a collage
of four most recent pictures.

00:01:51.650 --> 00:01:54.790
And you can see that
under the Collage tab.

00:01:54.790 --> 00:01:56.710
MARTIN: Oh, nice app.

00:01:56.710 --> 00:01:59.440
So can you tell us what's
happening behind the scenes

00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:03.170
and how you used this
choreography pattern?

00:02:03.170 --> 00:02:05.680
SPEAKER: Let's look at
that architecture diagram.

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:09.370
First, users upload pictures
to a Cloud Storage bucket

00:02:09.370 --> 00:02:11.940
using an App Engine frontend.

00:02:11.940 --> 00:02:15.180
That triggers an event
to a cloud function.

00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:17.940
This cloud function
uses Vision API

00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:21.150
to extract labels and
colors of the image

00:02:21.150 --> 00:02:23.790
and saves them to
Cloud Firestore.

00:02:23.790 --> 00:02:26.310
The same event triggers
a Cloud Run service

00:02:26.310 --> 00:02:30.540
that creates a
thumbnail of the image.

00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:32.450
There is a second
Cloud Run service

00:02:32.450 --> 00:02:34.820
that gets triggered
by Cloud Scheduler

00:02:34.820 --> 00:02:38.090
to create a collage of
four most-recent pictures

00:02:38.090 --> 00:02:40.270
on a schedule.

00:02:40.270 --> 00:02:42.640
Finally, a third
Cloud Run service

00:02:42.640 --> 00:02:44.830
gets triggered by
deletion events.

00:02:44.830 --> 00:02:48.600
It does some cleanup
after an image is deleted.

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:51.660
We are using built-in
events for cloud functions

00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:54.120
and Event [INAUDIBLE]
for Cloud Run.

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:56.310
The whole architecture
is event driven,

00:02:56.310 --> 00:02:58.920
also known as choreography.

00:02:58.920 --> 00:02:59.670
MARTIN: I see.

00:02:59.670 --> 00:03:03.640
So what are the pros and
cons of choreography?

00:03:03.640 --> 00:03:07.170
SPEAKER: Yeah, in choreography,
services are loosely coupled.

00:03:07.170 --> 00:03:09.000
And because they
are loosely coupled,

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:12.600
they can be changed and
scaled independently.

00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:15.180
If a service fails, the
rest of the services

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:17.310
can still send and
receive events,

00:03:17.310 --> 00:03:20.680
so there is no single
point of failure.

00:03:20.680 --> 00:03:22.770
And if you want to
add a new service

00:03:22.770 --> 00:03:26.040
or remove an existing
service to a new domain,

00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:28.720
eventing makes that really easy.

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:31.620
MARTIN: All right, so
then how do you monitor

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:33.380
event-driven systems like this?

00:03:33.380 --> 00:03:36.090
SPEAKER: Well, with all
the events flying around,

00:03:36.090 --> 00:03:39.570
it's actually quite hard to
monitor event-driven systems.

00:03:39.570 --> 00:03:43.620
It's also not so easy to catch
errors or apply global error

00:03:43.620 --> 00:03:44.810
and retry policies.

00:03:44.810 --> 00:03:46.770
MARTIN: Mmm, I see.

00:03:46.770 --> 00:03:49.410
And where is the
business logic captured?

00:03:49.410 --> 00:03:51.540
SPEAKER: Business logic
ends up being a side

00:03:51.540 --> 00:03:53.430
effect of the architecture.

00:03:53.430 --> 00:03:55.500
It's spread out to each service.

00:03:55.500 --> 00:04:00.510
And there is no single central
place that you can point to.

00:04:00.510 --> 00:04:01.140
MARTIN: Got it.

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:04.080
So can we fix
these cons somehow?

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:05.700
SPEAKER: Yes, we can.

00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:08.070
Need to keep the
loosely coupled nature

00:04:08.070 --> 00:04:11.700
of event-driven services,
but also bring some structure

00:04:11.700 --> 00:04:13.380
in the flow of events.

00:04:13.380 --> 00:04:15.690
We need some kind
of an orchestrator.

00:04:15.690 --> 00:04:18.810
Check out the next
episode for details.

00:04:18.810 --> 00:04:20.760
MARTIN: Cool,
Thanks, [INAUDIBLE]..

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:22.620
And thank you,
everyone, for watching.

00:04:22.620 --> 00:04:24.120
If you want to build
this app, there

00:04:24.120 --> 00:04:26.580
is a hands-on workshop
with a series of labs.

00:04:26.580 --> 00:04:28.980
The code is open source
on GitHub as well.

00:04:28.980 --> 00:04:31.200
All the links are
in the show notes.

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:31.800
Bye.

00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:35.450
[MUSIC PLAYING]

